      "writtenOn": "2020-06-30T01:55:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "OK can fix that.\nThe GPIO handling is a bit weird in terms of the UNUSED macro.\nIt puts the pins into an unused list, so I am not sure what happens if you\ntry to set them up as an interrupt afterwards.\nIt may be better to configure the input as normal, and then call gpio_reset on that pin if it is not going to be used.\nAlso, the GPIO_INT macro seems to put the pin in an interrupt table, and this wouldn\u0027t happen if the pin was UNUSED.",
      "parentUuid": "09bbb30d_3ed5ff86",
      "revId": "3183cd6749b6ab273b3155bfc71d183eaf6633f9",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f509ed39_937d2e4c",
        "filename": "board/puff/board.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 539,
      "author": {
        "id": 1361259
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-06-30T02:33:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The issue is just in having a floating input, so we could also configure it as an output by default.\n\nGPIO_OUT_LOW | GPIO_INT_BOTH by default, and reconfigure as an input if present. Defaulting to low won\u0027t fight with an external driver pulling the input low (it\u0027s on an open-drain net), and interrupts are still enabled.",
      "parentUuid": "82255bfa_9b259cee",
      "revId": "3183cd6749b6ab273b3155bfc71d183eaf6633f9",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
